cation  cycle  and at some point the  um the signal from that fluorescence  crosses  a threshold and at least in some  systems we call that threshold the cycle  threshold so it's the  amplification cycle number at which that  threshold for detection is reached and  the abbott systems  uh use a slightly different  thresholding measurement it's called the  fractional cycle number  or just cn or cycle number for short but  i'll  refer to that as a cycle threshold or  use those terms interchangeably the  the generation of the fractional cycle  numbers are actually very interesting  um and i'll refer to you  i'll refer you to the study by shane and  clements if you're interested in  more details about how that number is  determined  but um it is it's a way to  um uh sort of avoid some of the uh  artifacts and uh fluorescent of  baselining okay but we don't need to  know those uh details  just that we get this very important uh  cycle number information  the m2000 assay and the  uh alinity assay that i'll tell you  about later  they're both dual target assays so  they're detecting two targets within the  sars  cov2 virus and  importantly the assays have a robust  limit of detection  and so um as i mentioned  um real-time pcr is inherently  quantitative and so you know  our uh goal was then to be able to take  this  cycle threshold values and convert that  into a viral load  and see what that could tell us about  our testing results and our patient  population  so we determined that our limit of  detection  was um a hundred copies per  penum copies per mill um in  across our four different avid m2000  analyzers and the ct value was  approximately 26  and um you know as i mentioned uh  with pcr amplification if it's a 100  efficient you know that each cycle  number you're going to double  the number of amplicons so you go from 2  4 8 16  32 64 etc so  you can approximate the viral load so we  know  that for example that 100 is our lod  so you can use this sort of simple  formula 100 times  2 raised to the  limit of the ct at the limit of  detection minus  the ct for any given assay  to come up with an approximate borrower  load so  and that sort of makes sense because if  your ct was 25  that means you're basically one  your resulting one  cycle earlier so you would expect that  your viral load would be twice as high  as your limit of detection  if you if you plug that into the formula  you get a viral load of 200  if you're one cycle later then you're  limited detection  you plug it into the formula you get a a  bar load of 50.  well of course nothing in life is that  simple because  amplification efficiency is less than  100  and it decreases with cycle number and  you could these are graphs  of two positive samples  one with a very low ct and  one on the right with very high ct  near the limit of detection and you can  see that there's more  sort of the slope is steeper  with a a sample with a very high viral  load that gets detected  earlier and as you go through  additional amplification signals to  reach your  threshold the amplification becomes less  efficient  by basically looking at  a lot of different curves like this  we're able to empirically establish a  formula  which we think fairly accurately  predicts the viral load  and from this from the ct value and  we've  sort of published  some of this data now in bio  archive if you're interested in more  detail so  i'll just leave it that we think that we  have a pretty good approximation of  viral load  and so then we looked at  our uh data from march 26 to may  2nd and you know that was sort of our  big surge  in the state of massachusetts so we did  27 000  tests we had 6 000 positives 4 700  unique patients  and so we had two questions so for  a viral load to mean something one of  the questions was  well is it repeatable if you swab  someone's  nose and you get in twice in a row  and you've got a bar load of 100 one  time and 100 billion a second  then fire loads would no